<\body>
  <screens|<\hidden>
    <cqft-title-slide|<math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus I:<next-line>the FBSDE in force coordinates and renormalisation|Joint work with Sarah-Jean Meyer, with the use of AI|SISSA lectures, Lecture<nbsp>5 (provisional) \V notes: Lecture<nbsp>5, ŸŸ10\U12>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Where we are>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Lecture<nbsp>4.> Interacting field at resolution
    <math|t> = solution of the FBSDE

    <\equation*>
      \<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>.
    </equation*>

    In the <strong|forecast coordinate> <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>>
    the forward equation has no drift and the trees are integrated exactly;
    the backward half is the <strong|force equation>.

    <with|font-series|bold|Lectures<nbsp>5\U6.> Construct
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>> with this
    FBSDE:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><strong|deterministic renormalisation>: an explicit approximate
      force <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> and its defect (today);

      <item><strong|a priori estimates> for the exact FBSDE in the
      coordinates of <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>>, uniform in the
      lattice (Lecture<nbsp>6);

      <item><strong|removing the cutoffs> and identifying the limit
      (Lecture<nbsp>6).
    </enumerate>

    <with|par-mode|right|<small|<with|color|dark grey|Notes: Lectures<nbsp>5\U6 and the Supplement; Bailleul\UChevyrev\UG. (in prep.)>>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|What is proved>

    \;

    For every coupling <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>, every reference mass <math|q<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> and every finite part <math|r> of the counterterm, on a fixed torus, with no smallness,
    and an explicit counterterm with arbitrary finite part:

    <\itemize>
      <item>the whole lattice sequence converges to a measure <math|\<nu\>>,
      an explicit density over an explicit SDE;

      <item>the forecasts converge to the solution of an FBSDE from
      <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>, driven by the limit of the lattice
      innovations, and <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>=m<rsub|q><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>;

      <item>uniqueness in law for its scalar form, a variational formula with
      no infinite constant, quartic tails, integration by parts.
    </itemize>

    <strong|Not proved:> the vector FBSDE alone, strong solutions, OS axioms
    in full, infinite volume.

    <strong|Guiding principle.> Do not estimate <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|F>.
    Estimate the error of an explicit approximate forecast.

    The results of Lectures<nbsp>5\U6 are new: joint work with S.-J. Meyer +
    AI.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|The problem and the exact FBSDE>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Lattice and the mass-regulator profile>

    \;

    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N>=<around|(|N<rsup|-1>*\<bbb-Z\>/\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|3>>,
    <math|<big|int>f=N<rsup|-3>*<big|sum><rsub|x>f<around|(|x|)>>,
    <math|\<Delta\>=\<Delta\><rsub|N>> nearest neighbour. Resolution
    parameter <math|q=q<rsub|0>+t<rsup|2>\<in\><around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>>:

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|q>=q-\<Delta\>,<space|2em>C<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>C=C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    In Lecture<nbsp>3 notation (<math|T=\<infty\>>, and
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>=\<partial\><rsub|q>*Q<rsub|q>>):

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|q>=C-C<rsub|q>,<space|1em><wide|Q|\<dot\>>=C<rsub|q><rsup|2>,<space|1em><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>=C<rsub|q>,<space|1em>S<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>,<space|1em>\<cal-R\><rsub|q>=q-q<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Free resolvents (uniform in <math|N>)>

      <math|C<rsub|q>\<geqslant\>0> as a kernel,
      <math|C<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=q<rsup|-1>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>=q<rsup|-1>>,

      <\equation*>
        a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>=<around|(|C<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)><around|(|x,x|)>\<lesssim\>q<rsup|-1/2>\<wedge\>N<rsup|3>*q<rsup|-2>,<space|2em><math-up|Tr>C\<asymp\>N.
      </equation*>
    </myframed>

    The <strong|bubble> <math|a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>> is the
    coincident-point variance of the shell at resolution <math|q>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The cutoff measure>

    <math|U<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4><big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|4>>,
    <math|V<rsub|N>=U+<frac|\<mu\><rsub|N>|2><big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|2>>,
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|N><around|(|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=\<cal-Z\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|C><around|(|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>>.

    <math|\<gamma\>=3\<lambda\>>, so <math|D<rsup|2>U<around|(|y|)>=\<gamma\>
    y<rsup|2>> (not the counterterm <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>> of
    Lecture<nbsp>4).\ 

    The Wick ordering is now part of <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>.

    <strong|Reference mass> <math|q<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> (constants
    <math|\<in\>ker \<Delta\>> on the torus; <math|r> may be negative).
    Only <math|q<rsub|0>+\<mu\><rsub|N>> matters: fix <math|q<rsub|0>>,
    vary <math|r>.


    <\myframed>
      <strong|Main result, informal.> Let

      <\equation*>
        \<mu\><rsub|N>=r-3\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>
        C+6\<lambda\><rsup|2><big|int> C<rsup|3>.
      </equation*>

      Then (a) <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>\<to\>\<nu\>> in <math|H<rsup|-s>>,
      <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>, along the whole sequence; (b) the forecasts
      converge in law to a solution of a limiting FBSDE; (c) <math|\<nu\>>
      has a variational formula, an integration by parts formula and quartic
      tails.
    </myframed>

    These come from bounds on the error <math|Z> of an approximate forecast,
    uniform in <math|N>: <strong|UV stability>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The Polchinski process as a filter>

    By Lecture<nbsp>4, in the profile <math|S<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>>,
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|q>=q-q<rsub|0>>; at fixed <math|N> everything is a
    theorem.<space|1em>The Polchinski process solves

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=C<rsub|q><rsup|2>F<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+C<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,<space|1em>F<rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q><around|[|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>,<space|1em>\<Phi\><rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0,
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\<to\>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|N>>.

    <\itemize>
      <item>a noisy observation of its endpoint,
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>C<rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>+C<rsub|q>B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>>,
      with <math|B> independent of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>;

      <item>forecast

      <\equation*>
        \<psi\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=\<Phi\><rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>F<rsub|q>=m<rsub|q><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>,
      </equation*>

      covariance <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>\<gtr\>0>;

      <item><math|W> is the innovation: <math|\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>>;

      <item>noise: <math|\<eta\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|q>-<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>\<psi\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>>,
      sub-Gaussian whatever the interaction (L4).
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|One site>

    \;

    <image|<tuple|<#>|onesite_filter.pdf>|1par|||>

    One site, <math|\<nu\>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>/4>\<mu\><rsup|1/q<rsub|0>>>,
    <math|\<lambda\>=2>, <math|q<rsub|0>=1>. Left: one path of the
    observation <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>> and of the forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>, both converging to the hidden
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>. Right, over 200 paths: the forecast
    error <math|\<sim\>q<rsup|-1/2>>, the force term
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>F<rsub|q>\<sim\>q<rsup|-1>>,
    and the error <math|Z<rsub|q>> of the approximate forecast from
    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>=\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>>, below the bound
    <math|2C<rsub|\<ast\>>\<lambda\><rsup|1/2>q<rsup|-3/2>> of Theorem<nbsp>12.8.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The exact force in the forecast coordinate>

    Since <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\<mapsto\>\<psi\><rsub|q>> is invertible,
    define <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>> by

    <\equation*>
      \<Phi\><rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>\<cal-F\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>
    </equation*>

    and <math|H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+D\<cal-F\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    It is the <math|\<cal-F\>> of Lecture<nbsp>4
    (<math|x=\<psi\>+S\<cal-F\><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>), and
    <math|F<rsub|q>=-\<cal-F\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>>.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|The force equation with a mass regulator>

      <\equation*>
        \<partial\><rsub|q>\<cal-F\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Ctr><rsub|<around|(|H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|2>>D<rsup|2>\<cal-F\><rsub|q>=0,<space|1em><math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>.
      </equation*>
    </myframed>

    \;

    Itô on <math|\<Phi\>=\<psi\>+C\<cal-F\><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>. Its
    integrated form is Wetterich's equation, which we do not need. An
    identity about the unknown <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>, not a construction.\ 

    <with|font-series|bold|Strategy:> an approximate force
    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>> gives approximate forecast coordinates;
    the error should be driven by the failure of
    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>> to solve this equation.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Approximate forecast and error>

    \;

    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> odd,
    <math|D<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> symmetric,
    <math|K<rsub|q>+D<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<geqslant\><wide|c|\<bar\>><rsub|q>>.

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|q>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+D<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>
      \<cal-D\><rsub|q>=\<partial\><rsub|q><wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Ctr><rsub|H<rsub|q><rsup|2>>D<rsup|2><wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>,<space|1em>
      b<rsub|q>=-H<rsub|q>\<cal-D\><rsub|q>.
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      Y<rsub|q>+C<rsub|q><wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|q>,<space|1em>
      Z<rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q>-Y<rsub|q>,<space|1em>\<Xi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>-H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Exact, at fixed> <math|N>

      <\equation*>
        <space|0.17em>\<mathd\> Y<rsub|q>=<around|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>Z<rsub|q>|]><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
        q+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W<rsub|q>,
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        <space|0.17em>\<mathd\> Z<rsub|q>=-<around|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>Z<rsub|q>|]><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
        q+\<Xi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W<rsub|q>,<space|1em>
        Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=Z<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0.
      </equation*>
    </myframed>

    Proof: Itô on the coordinate equation, with the Polchinski process and <math|\<mathd\>C<rsub|q>=-C<rsub|q><rsup|2>\<mathd\>q>: the
    martingale part gives <math|H>, the drift gives <math|H*Z+b>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Reading the system>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|Y>: forward and explicit once <math|Z> is known;
      coefficients <math|b,H> built from <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>>.

      <item><math|Z>: backward. The terminal condition
      <math|q*Z<rsub|q>\<to\>0> is <strong|proved> at fixed <math|N>
      (Lecture<nbsp>6), because <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<to\>>
      bare force.

      <item>The only source of <math|Z> is the drift <math|b>, the force
      defect, transported by <math|exp <big|int>H>.
    </itemize>

    \;

    <\myframed>
      <strong|UV stability>

      The explicit force <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>> computes the
      forecast up to an error <math|Z> which is small uniformly in <math|N>.
    </myframed>

    \;

    Needed: (i) a defect integrable against the backward propagator
    (<strong|renormalisation>); (ii) global coercivity of
    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>> (<strong|large fields>); (iii) a priori
    estimates (Lecture<nbsp>6).
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The structure of the construction>

    \;

    \;

