  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Two correction steps>

    \;

    Backward propagator <math|\<approx\>s/q>, so
    <math|Z<rsub|q>\<approx\><big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|s|q>*b<rsub|s>\<mathd\>s>,
    and on the ball <math|<around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<lesssim\>s<rsup|-1><math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|s>|)>*r<rsub|s><rsup|3>>.
    After <math|n> steps, <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|s>|)>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|s><rsup|n+1>*s<rsup|1/2>*r<rsub|s><rsup|-4>>:

    \;

    <center|<data-table|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|steps
    <math|n>>|<cell|<math|<math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|s>|)>>>|<cell|<math|<around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>>>|<cell|<math|<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|s|q><around|\||b<rsub|s>|\|>\<mathd\>s>>>|<row|<cell|<math|0>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|3>*s<rsup|-2>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|3/2>*s<rsup|-3/2>>>|<cell|<math|\<infty\>>
    (power)>>|<row|<cell|<math|1>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|4>*s<rsup|-5/2>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|5/2>*s<rsup|-2>>>|<cell|<math|\<infty\>>
    (log)>>|<row|<cell|<math|2>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|5>*s<rsup|-3>>>|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*s<rsup|-5/2>>>|<cell|<math|2*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-3/2>>>>>>>>

    \;

    \;

    <strong|Two steps are the minimum, and suffice.> The defect of one step
    is borderline in the backward equation, as the sunset is borderline in
    the mass. (Heuristic threshold for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>:
    <math|n\<gtr\>1>; the general theory's sufficient criterion is
    <math|<around|(|n+2|)>*\<varkappa\>\<gtr\><around|(|3+\<varkappa\>|)>/2>,
    <math|\<varkappa\>=<frac|1|2>>, met by <math|n=2>.)
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|The flow at large fields>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The large-field problem>

    \;

    All bounds hold on <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|q>> only. The free field exceeds
    <math|r<rsub|q>> with small, nonzero probability, and nothing a priori
    controls the interacting forecast. We need
    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> defined and uniformly coercive
    for all fields.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Scalar model>

      <\equation*>
        \<ell\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=\<gamma\>
        y<around|[|<around|(|q+\<gamma\> y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>-q<rsup|-1>|]>
      </equation*>

      : power series of radius exactly <math|r<rsub|q>> (singularities at
      <math|\<pm\>\<mathi\> r<rsub|q>>). Half of the truncations destroy
      monotonicity. The exact function is harmless,
      <math|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|q>'|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|9|8>\<gamma\>/q>.
    </myframed>

    \;

    Perturbative control for <math|<around|\||y|\|>\<lesssim\> r<rsub|q>>;
    outside, a non-perturbative replacement that keeps the structure the
    estimates use: coercivity, carried by the bare cube.

    One site: <math|q*y+\<lambda\> y<rsup|3>=q*\<Phi\><rsub|q>>, noise coefficient
    <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|q+\<gamma\>
    y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\> q<rsup|-1>\<wedge\><around|(|\<gamma\>
    y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>. Large fields are more elliptic and less noisy.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Clipping>

    \;

    <math|\<tau\>> smooth odd, <math|\<tau\><around|(|u|)>=u> on
    <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>, constant outside <math|<around|[|-2,2|]>>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<tau\>'\<leqslant\>1>;

    <\equation*>
      \<kappa\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=r<rsub|q>\<tau\><around|(|y/r<rsub|q>|)>,<space|1em><wide|\<ell\>|~><rsub|q>=D\<kappa\><rsub|q>\<cdot\>\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|2|]>><rsub|q>\<circ\>\<kappa\><rsub|q>,<space|1em><wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=U'+M<rsub|q>y+<wide|\<ell\>|~><rsub|q>.
    </equation*>

    The cubic and linear parts are not clipped, and <math|D\<kappa\><rsub|q>>
    keeps the force a gradient. Globally:
    <math|s<around|(|D<rsup|j-1><wide|\<ell\>|~><rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>
    C*g<rsub|q>q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-j>> (<math|j=2,3,4>),
    <math|s<around|(|D\<partial\><rsub|q><wide|\<ell\>|~><rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>
    C*g<rsub|q>>.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Global coercivity>

      For all <math|y>: <math|c<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>
      y<rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\> H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>
      C<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\> y<rsup|2>|)>>,

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>
        p>\<leqslant\><frac|1+C<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>|q>,<space|1em><math-up|diag>H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>
        C*a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>.
      </equation*>
    </myframed>

    Neumann series for <math|H=<around|(|I+G<around|(|M+D<wide|\<ell\>|~>|)>|)><rsup|-1>G>,
    <math|G=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\> y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>
    C<rsub|q>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The complete defect>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      \;

      <math|b=b<rsup|<math-up|b>>+b<rsup|<math-up|l>>>,
      <math|b<rsup|<math-up|b>>=-H*<space|0.17em>D*\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-D\><rsup|<math-up|o>>*<around|(|\<kappa\>*y|)>>,
      <math|t<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=y*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>>>.

      <\proposition*>
        <dueto|uniformly in <math|y>, <math|N>, <math|p>>

        <\equation*>
          <around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>+r<rsub|q><rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|y<rsup|2>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>|)>,
        </equation*>

        <\equation*>
          <around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>.
        </equation*>
      </proposition*>

      Sources of <math|b<rsup|<math-up|l>>> (four groups): the mass term
      <math|M<rprime|'>*y-J*M<rprime|'>*\<kappa\>*y> (<math|J=D*\<kappa\>>,
      <math|M<rprime|'>=\<partial\><rsub|q>*M>; linear part unclipped, flow
      computed at <math|\<kappa\>*y>); the motion of the clip; cubic vertex
      at <math|y> vs correction at <math|\<kappa\>*y>; chain-rule terms with
      <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''>,\<kappa\><rprime|'''>>. All vanish if
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|q>>.

      <\myframed>
        <strong|The point>

        <math|b<rsup|<math-up|l>>> is as large as the whole drift, but
        <strong|supported on large fields>. It is controlled by how often the
        actual dynamics visits them: <math|<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q>
        (Lecture<nbsp>6).
      </myframed>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The coordinate equation>

    \;

    With <math|D*U=<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*y<rsup|3>>:

    <strong|Up to a small Lipschitz perturbation, the forecast solves the
    cubic elliptic equation>

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*<around*|(|Y<rsub|q>+\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*Y<rsub|q><rsup|3>,<space|2em>\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=C<rsub|q>*<around*|(|M<rsub|q>*y+<wide|\<ell\>|~><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<varrho\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>.
    </equation*>

    driven by the field at resolution <math|q>.

    <with|font-series|bold|Summary of today>, at every lattice:

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>, <math|M<rsub|q>> and a globally coercive
      <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>>, all deterministic;

      <item>the exact FBSDE for <math|<around|(|Y,Z|)>>;

      <item>a source whose bounded-field part is integrable (two steps), and
      whose large-field part is controlled by sixth powers of <math|Y> on
      large fields.
    </itemize>

    <with|font-series|bold|Next:> estimates on <math|<around|(|Y,Z|)>>
    uniform in <math|N>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The force below the auxiliary scale>

    \;

    The expansion needs <math|g<rsub|q>> small, so
    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> is built for <math|q\<geqslant\>
    q<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Below <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> nothing needs
    constructing: freeze it,

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|y|)>+<around|(|q<rsub|\<ast\>>-q|)>y.
    </equation*>

    Then <math|K<rsub|q>+D<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>+D<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>:
    coercivity is automatic, <math|H<rsub|q>=H<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>, and
    <math|y<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>=y<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|C<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>K<rsub|q>x|)>>.

    The defect is explicit: <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|s><around|(|y|)>=-y+<frac|1|2><math-up|Ctr><rsub|H<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|2>>D<rsup|2><wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|y|)>>,
    with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-D\><rsub|s><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>
    C<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>.

    So the system holds on <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>> with
    <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>: the initial value is deterministic, with no
    large mass. <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> stays only in the proofs, where the
    expansion and the closure of the loop need <math|g<rsub|q>> small.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Why not Polchinski with a frozen cube?>

    \;

    <strong|Programme:> keep <math|-\<lambda\>\<varphi\><rsup|3>> unclipped,
    clip the higher orders, pay in the source of <math|R=F-f>. Test it on one
    site: <math|K<rsub|q>=q>, <math|S=1/q>.

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>=-\<lambda\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>Y<rsup|3>=q<around|(|m<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>-x|)>,<space|1em><around|\||m<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2\<lambda\><rsup|-1/4>+2<around|(|q<around|\||x|\|>/\<lambda\>|)><rsup|1/3>.
    </equation*>

